## Introduction

The same tiles, the same room, the same budget — and the pattern alone changes everything about how the space reads. Straight grid lays calm and formal; diagonal layouts visually expand tight rooms; herringbone adds movement and craft; running bond hides the size variations in cheaper tiles.

This guide covers the main tile layout patterns: what each does to a room's perceived proportions, the cutting waste each pattern demands (and what that does to your bill), the tile formats each pattern suits, and the marking-out method that keeps the geometry honest from the first tile to the last cut.

## The Grammar of Patterns: Reading Before Choosing

Every layout pattern is a trade among three things, and knowing the trade is choosing well:

**Sight-line discipline.** Grid patterns expose the room's edges: every grout line runs parallel to a wall, so an out-of-square wall shows up as a wedge of a joint. Patterns that break the sight-line (diagonal, herringbone, Versailles) hide wall deviations and imperfections in the substrate plane — which is why they read as more "forgiving."

**Perceived proportion.** Straight grid lines pull the eye along the room's axis, lengthening it. Diagonals pull the eye toward the corners, which widens a narrow room's feel. Small-format patterns in busy arrangements compress; large-format grids in straight lines expand. The pattern is a visual tool applied to the room's actual geometry.

**Waste and labour.** Complexity is paid for twice: in cutting waste (straight lay 5–10%, diagonal 10–15%, herringbone 15–20%) and in labour hours (herringbone and Versailles layouts can double or triple the fixing time of a straight grid). The bill follows the geometry — see [our cost estimation guide](/learn/tile-cost-estimation-materials-labour-budget) for how waste factors feed the materials block.

With the grammar clear, the individual patterns:

## Straight Grid (Stack Bond): Calm, Formal, Demanding

**The look.** Tiles aligned in a strict grid — every joint continuing in an unbroken line across the room. The most formal, most architectural of the simple patterns; reads as deliberate, gallery-like, and contemporary in large formats.

**Where it works.** Rectified porcelain in large formats (600 × 600 and up) in modern interiors; feature areas where the design wants severity; walls in any format where a calm backdrop is the goal.

**The catch.** Stack bond is the least forgiving pattern there is: continuous joint lines highlight every tile that is a fraction out, and the strict alignment offers nowhere to hide calibration variance. It demands rectified, well-calibrated tiles, a near-perfect substrate, and a tiler who holds tolerance through hundreds of repetitions. With standard (non-rectified) tiles it is a pattern that sues its own installer — the cheaper the tile, the crueler the grid.

**The waste.** Lowest of any pattern: 5–8%. Perimeter cuts are simple parallels, and offcuts from one row often serve the row opposite.

**Marking-out.** A true centre cross (3-4-5 method), a dry-lay to balance the cuts, and continuous straightedge checks every few rows — the full set-out method is in [our floor installation guide](/learn/complete-guide-floor-tile-installation).

## Running Bond (Brick/Offset): The Everyday Professional

**The look.** Each row offset by half a tile from the last, like brickwork. The classic, quietly busy pattern of most quality floors — it carries visual movement without drama, and its broken joint lines forgive far more than stack bond.

**Where it works.** Almost everywhere: any rectangular format, any room, any tile quality. Running bond is the default recommendation for standard ceramic with ordinary calibration, because the offset absorbs the size variance that a strict grid would expose. It suits wood-look planks particularly well — indeed, wood-look tiles exist to be laid this way.

**The catch — and it is a specific one.** The offset percentage matters. The traditional 50% offset, on large rectified tiles, puts every tile's centre on another tile's joint — and the slightly cupped edge of large-format tiles then produces lippage precisely at the weakest point of the row. Modern large-format practice uses a 30–40% offset (a "third bond" is common) instead of 50%, specifically to dodge the worst-case alignment of tile-edge cupping. On small and medium formats, 50% is fine and traditional; over 600 mm, drop the offset.

**The waste.** 8–10%. The offset cuts are slightly less reusable than grid cuts, and the row-start halves must be maintained.

**Marking-out.** Establish the perpendicular starting line, then a gauge rod (a marked stick or story pole with tile-plus-joint increments) to plot each row's start offset — a physical gauge beats arithmetic on your knees, every time.

## Diagonal (Diamond): The Space Widener

**The look.** The grid rotated 45°, so joints cross the room at the diagonal instead of parallel to walls. The room reads wider — the eye follows the diagonal into the corners rather than down the length.

**Where it works.** Small rooms, hallways, and awkward spaces that read narrow; entrance halls that want presence; any room whose proportions need flattering. It also de-emphasizes out-of-square walls, because no joint runs parallel to them.

**The catch.** Every perimeter cut is a diagonal cut — more cuts, longer cuts, more blade time, more breakage at the points. Diagonal layouts also concentrate small triangular slivers at the walls; plan the field position so the slivers land at the least-seen edges. Labour rises measurably: expect the tiler's rate to reflect it.

**The waste.** 10–15% — plan the higher figure for rooms with many corners and obstacles. This feeds the bill directly: a 15%-waste diagonal layout in a 12 m² room needs roughly two more square metres of tile than an 8%-waste grid — the pattern choice, priced.

**Marking-out.** The reference is a diagonal, not a wall: snap two lines corner-to-corner across the room centre to find the true centre point, then strike a perpendicular, then a 45° diagonal through the centre with the 3-4-5 method applied to the rotated frame. Dry-lay the first quadrant before committing; the first tile's angle decides every subsequent one.

## Herringbone: Movement, Craft, and Its Price

**The look.** Rectangular tiles laid in interlocking L-shaped zigzags — the pattern that turns a floor into a feature. Herringbone reads as craft and expense because it historically was both; it draws the eye along its woven rhythm and suits both classic and contemporary interiors depending on the tile.

**Where it works.** Hallways and entrances (its natural habitat — the zigzag leads the eye forward); feature floors of kitchens and living rooms; feature walls. Rectangular formats only: the classic ratio is 2:1 (a 150 × 75 or 200 × 100 tile), and modern large-format herringbone (300 × 600 rotated) gives the same rhythm at a larger scale.

**The catch.** Herringbone is geometrically unforgiving: every tile meets another at a precise angle, and a small error at the start propagates visibly down the field. It demands rectified tiles (the pattern multiplies edge variance), careful set-out from a true centre, and either an experienced tiler or a very patient one. The fixing is roughly double the time of a grid, and 45° cuts at the perimeter are numerous.

**The waste.** 15–20%. On large floors, that is a real line in the budget — and herringbone also punishes under-buying: matching a batch mid-pattern, mid-floor, is the worst possible time to discover the shade codes differ.

**Marking-out.** The professional method starts at the room centre with two perpendicular lines, then establishes the herringbone's axis along one of them: the first "L" pair is set precisely on the axis, and the pattern grows outward in both directions. A gauge rod marked with the pattern's repeat keeps the rhythm honest as the field expands — and dry-laying the first metre, and standing back to look at it, catches angle errors while they are still one-tile deep.

**A note on chevron**, herringbone's cousin: chevron tiles are cut at an angle and point to make a continuous zigzag rather than a broken one. It looks similar at a glance and demands factory-angled tiles — field-cutting chevrons is a losing battle. If the look is wanted, buy it pre-cut.

## Modular (Hopscotch/Versailles): The Multi-Format Weave

**The look.** Two or more tile sizes combined in a repeating weave — 450s alternating with 225s in the hopscotch pattern; three to four sizes in a French/Versailles pattern. The result reads as organic and expensive, with visual depth a single-format floor cannot match.

**Where it works.** Living spaces that want texture without a strong directional pattern; stone-look porcelain in particular benefits, because the multi-format weave mimics traditional stone flooring; exteriors and driveways in the larger versions.

**The catch.** The pattern repeat must be laid out correctly from the first tile — modular patterns have a defined module that repeats, and breaking it mid-room is visible forever. The sizes must also be genuinely modular (from the same manufacturer's calibrated family, made to nest at the stated joints); improvising a modular pattern from mismatched sizes produces joint lines that wander instead of weave. Sorting and staging the different sizes adds a whole logistics layer: the tiler lays from a planned sequence of tile sizes, not from open boxes.

**The waste.** 10–15%. The module repeats exactly, so once the perimeter strategy is set, waste is predictable — but the sorting labour is part of the cost.

**Marking-out.** Lay out one full module on the dry floor (the manufacturer's pattern sheet gives the arrangement), confirm its total dimensions divide the room sensibly, and set out the module grid — not individual tiles — across the space. Everything follows the module.

## Choosing by Room and by Budget: The Summary Table

| Pattern | Waste | Labour | Forgiveness | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Straight grid | 5–8% | Baseline | Lowest | Large rectified formats, formal interiors |
| Running bond | 8–10% | Low | Good | The everyday default; standard tiles; planks |
| Diagonal | 10–15% | Medium | Good | Small/narrow rooms, halls, entrances |
| Herringbone | 15–20% | High | Low | Feature floors, hallways, craft interiors |
| Modular | 10–15% | Medium | Medium | Multi-format weaves, stone-look, exteriors |

**The budget translation.** A 12 m² living room with ₦8,000/m² tiles: the grid needs ~13 m² of tile, the diagonal ~14 m², the herringbone ~14.5 m² plus doubled fixing hours. Same tiles, same room — the pattern is the difference between two budgets, and knowing that in the planning stage is the point of this guide. The full quantity method, including waste factors by pattern, is in [our tile calculation guide](/learn/how-to-calculate-tile-quantities-any-room).

## The Marking-Out Method That Governs Them All

Whatever the pattern, the geometry is kept honest by the same four-step discipline:

1. **Find truth, not walls.** Reference lines come from the 3-4-5 method and a snapped cross at the room's true centre — never from a wall assumed square.
2. **Dry-lay the critical first metre.** The first module, the first herringbone L, the first diagonal row: laid dry, viewed standing up, corrected while correction costs nothing.
3. **Use a gauge rod for every repeating pattern.** Tile-plus-joint increments, physically marked, checked against the field every few rows — the rod catches drift that the eye forgives and the room's far corner then displays.
4. **Plan the perimeter before the centre is finished.** Where the cuts land is a design decision made at set-out; discovering sliver cuts when the field reaches the wall is how "almost" enters a floor's biography.

The full preparation sequence these steps sit inside — substrate, set-out, adhesive, laying — is the [complete floor installation guide](/learn/complete-guide-floor-tile-installation), and the spacing and alignment habits that keep any pattern true are detailed in [our common tiling mistakes guide](/learn/common-tiling-mistakes-and-how-to-avoid).

## Conclusion

Patterns are the grammar of a tiled room: the grid states, the running bond narrates, the diagonal flatters, the herringbone performs, and the modular weave composes. Each demands a known waste factor, a known labour profile, and a marking-out method matched to its geometry — and every one of them succeeds on the same foundation of true reference lines, dry-laid beginnings, and a gauge rod instead of optimism.

Choose the pattern for the room's proportions and the tile's quality, price the pattern's waste and hours honestly, and lay it by its own geometry's rules. Then the pattern does what the best of them do — make a good floor look inevitable.
